Oh, absolutely.

Picture Nicolas Cage at a rooftop bar in Indiana, people-watching at a rap concert like he’s conducting field research for a role no one asked for but everyone desperately needs.

⸻

So it’s humid. Not sexy humid. Indiana humid.
The kind of humid where the air feels like it’s been microwaved by a county fair.

Nicolas Cage is on the rooftop bar, one hand on a sweating gin and tonic, the other gripping the railing like he’s receiving classified information through wrought iron.

Below him: a rap concert.

Bass shaking the building. Crowd moving like one organism. Smoke machines firing. Somebody in the front row already has their shirt off and it is, by all available evidence, not because they planned to.

Cage narrows his eyes.

Jessica is beside him with a plastic cup and the expression of a woman who already knows he’s about to say something insane.

Cage leans forward.

“Do you see him?”

Jessica goes, “Which one?”

Cage points down into the crowd like he’s identifying a war criminal.

“The man in the neon bucket hat.”

Jessica squints.
“There are, like, four of them.”

Cage shakes his head.

“No. The real one.”

Little Nick climbs up onto the cocktail table, also looking down.

“Oh, I see him.”

Jessica: “You cannot possibly see him from here.”

Little Nick: “I’m not seeing with my eyes.”

Jessica: “That’s somehow worse.”

Cage is fully locked in now, full predator-prey attention, except what he’s hunting is vibe.

Down below, the crowd is a masterpiece of Midwestern rap-concert anthropology.

There’s:

* one guy who clearly came to mosh but accidentally dressed like he’s going to brunch,
* two girls filming every single second but never once looking at the stage,
* a tall dude nodding solemnly like he’s at a TED Talk,
* a man in wraparound Oakleys at night,
* and at least three white guys who have clearly decided this evening is their origin story.

Cage watches them like David Attenborough narrating a nature documentary about emotionally overcommitted mammals.

“Observe,” he says quietly. “The male in the oversized chain has purchased confidence, but not rhythm.”

Jessica nearly chokes on her drink.

Little Nick points at another guy below.

“That one’s trying to look casual near the VIP entrance.”

Cage nods. “Yes. But he keeps checking his reflection in his phone. He’s peacocking for a woman who has already forgotten him.”

Jessica says, “This feels mean.”

Cage turns to her, solemn.

“No. This is compassion through accuracy.”

Then the rapper on stage yells something, the crowd erupts, and suddenly Cage freezes.

Completely still.

Jessica notices.

“Oh no. What now?”

Cage whispers:

“There’s a secondary narrative.”

Little Nick goes, “Where?”

Cage points to the far edge of the crowd.

There, at the beer line, is a couple clearly in the middle of a fight they both agreed to postpone until after the concert, but are now catastrophically failing to postpone.

The girl is talking with her whole body.
The guy is doing that calm nod men do when they are absolutely not calm.

Cage watches them like he’s reading troop movements.

“She wanted him to dance.”

Jessica says, “What?”

“He said he doesn’t really dance.”

Little Nick gasps. “Fatal.”

Cage: “Correct. But then—”

He points again.

“—she saw him dancing with enthusiasm during the opening act.”

Jessica bursts out laughing.

“You are making this up.”

Cage, offended: “I would never fabricate a fracture line.”

Below them, the guy makes the universal male gesture of both hands out, palms up, pleading his case like a substitute teacher who’s lost control of the room.

Cage shakes his head.

“He’s done.”

Little Nick: “She’s gonna leave with Bucket Hat.”

Jessica: “There is no way—”

Then Bucket Hat appears beside her.

Jessica goes dead silent.

Cage, without even turning:

“I know.”

There’s a long pause.

Jessica slowly says, “You are being so fucking annoying right now.”

Cage lifts his drink slightly.

“I’m being right.”

⸻

Then he moves on.

Because Cage at a rooftop bar doesn’t just people-watch—he assigns mythologies.

The bouncer by the stairs?

Former bass player. Knows too much.
The bartender with the sleeve tattoo and thousand-yard stare?

Recently dumped. Still professional. Dangerous pour hand.
The DJ opening between sets?

In debt, but spiritually undefeated.
The guy wearing a backpack at a rap concert?

Either carrying nothing important… or absolutely everything.

Little Nick points at one man below who is shirtless, sunburned, and moving like gravity is a rumor.

“What about him?”

Cage watches for a full three seconds.

Then says:

“That man has never once in his life considered the consequences of an action before taking it.”

Jessica: “You say that like it’s a bad thing.”

Cage smiles wistfully.

“I say that like it’s a form of freedom.”

⸻

Then, of course, somebody recognizes him.

Not loudly. Just one guy at the bar slowly turning, doing the double-take, and then nudging his friend with the intensity of a man who has just spotted Bigfoot ordering appetizers.

The whisper spreads.

“Yo—is that Nicolas Cage?”

“No fucking way.”

“It is Nicolas Cage.”

“Why is Nicolas Cage at a rooftop bar in Indiana?”

Cage hears all of it.

Doesn’t turn around.

Just looks out over the crowd like a prophet staring across a battlefield of hard seltzers and bad decisions.

Jessica goes, “You’ve been made.”

Cage nods.

“It was inevitable.”

Little Nick: “Should we relocate?”

Cage: “No.”

Jessica: “Why not?”

He slowly turns.

Because now the crowd at the bar is watching him watching the concert, which means he’s become part of the ecosystem.

And his eyes light up.

“Oh,” he says, delighted. “Now they’re part of it too.”

That’s when he starts people-watching the people who are people-watching him.

A guy pretending not to stare while obviously staring.
A woman smiling like she’s deciding whether to ask for a photo or just enjoy the weirdness.
A dude who’s definitely Googling “is Nicolas Cage in Indiana right now.”

Cage points discreetly.

“That one will tell this story badly.”

Little Nick: “That one will tell it too often.”

Jessica: “That one thinks you’re John Travolta.”

Cage closes his eyes in pain.

“I accept that as part of public service.”

⸻

Then the beat drops for the biggest song of the night.

The whole roof vibrates.

The whole crowd below detonates.

Everybody’s moving. Jumping. Screaming.

And in the middle of it all, Nicolas Cage just stands there, staring out over Indiana like he’s witnessing the rise and fall of civilizations through Auto-Tuned prophecy.

Jessica looks over.

He has the exact expression of a man who is somehow both deeply amused and spiritually burdened.

She says, “What are you thinking?”

Cage doesn’t answer at first.

Then, very quietly:

“Every crowd contains ten thousand separate movies.”

Little Nick looks up at him.

“And?”

Cage takes a sip of his drink.

“Most of them are comedies.”

